KNOWNBUG
sequence_not2.sv
--bound 0
^\[main\.p0\] always not strong\(main\.a ##1 main\.b\): PROVED up to bound 0$
^\[main\.p1\] always not weak\(main\.a ##1 main\.b\): PROVED up to bound 0$
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
--
property_obligations_rec (src/trans-word-level/property.cpp) handles
"not sequence" by requiring that no match of the sequence occurs,
including pending matches, i.e., matches that cannot complete within
the bound.  A pending match is inconclusive, and is no evidence for a
match, hence must not refute the negated sequence.  EBMC reports
REFUTED with a one-state counterexample.  Note that the same defect
also affects "not sequence" used as an assumption, where the pending
match is then wrongly ruled out, which makes the assumption stronger
than intended.  The sva_or1 KNOWNBUG test is likely another instance.
